A||||5||NC86GT||Front Control Arm Bushings||||8||
Z||000000||NC86GT||10-14-2001||01:11 AM||||I'm restoring my fiero and I have the front end apart.  What is the best way to get the bushings out to replace them.  I got the rubber out, now do I have to take out that metal sleeve too. TIA||||reg||8
Z||000001||Tigger||10-14-2001||01:35 AM||||Read up on the tips for control arm bushings at <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/ogre/" TARGET=_blank>TheOgre's Cave</A>.<P>He recommends drilling into the rubber until you can press them out by hand or knock them out with a hammer.  Some heat the outer sleve like I did and knocked them out, dangerous but got the job done.<P>Don't have to remove the outer sleeve if the new bushings didn't come with them.  BTW, are you replacing with poly or rubber?||||reg||1
Z||000002||SKIDMARK||10-14-2001||11:51 AM||||If you have to remove the metal sleeves (I've never seen bushings come without them but I could easily be wrong), you have to support the control arm flange on both sides. Otherwise you'll crush the control arm. I used a press and some pipe to push the sleeves out. I cut blocks of wood that fit between the two sides of the flange adjacent to the sleeve so I wouldn't crush the sides of each flange together. I did the same thing when pressing the new bushings in. You can also take the time to slot the sleeves with a hacksaw. This makes them easy to remove without doing any damage but you stil have to press or hammer the new ones in. Not hard, just time consuming. Makes a big difference in handling though!<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||4
Z||000003||Formula88||10-14-2001||12:50 PM||||Most poly bushing sets require the re-use of the outer sleeve from the old rubber bushings, so make sure you check to see if you need them before proceeding.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||roadwarrior||10-14-2001||06:14 PM||||burn them out with some oxy/acetalyne or a propane torch.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||10-14-2001||06:24 PM||||My poly bushings didn't have an outer sleeve.  I heated them up until the glue let go, then pulled the inner sleeve out with pliers, then pushed the rubber out with a pipe.  Real simple.  Then I hit them with some 600 grit wet/dry sand paper, lubed them up and installed the bushings.  It's been over 6 years with no problems.||||reg||1
